   Khai-Duy and Iizuka, Tetsuya and Hoang, Trong-Thuc and Pham, Cong-Kha},
Book-Group-Author = {IEEE},
Title = {A Unified OTP and PUF Exploiting Post-Program Current on Standard CMOS
   Technology},
Booktitle = {2024 IEEE INTERNATIONAL SYMPOSIUM ON CIRCUITS AND SYSTEMS, ISCAS 2024},
Series = {IEEE International Symposium on Circuits and Systems},
Year = {2024},
Note = {IEEE International Symposium on Circuits and Systems (ISCAS), Singapore,
   SINGAPORE, MAY 19-22, 2024},
Abstract = {Root-of-Trust (RoT) uses the hardware primitives to provide security in
   the integrated electronic systems, preventing attacks against the vital
   information of the system. The typical hardware primitives are used to
   generate random numbers and store the system's keys. However, the
   implementation of these primitives achieves challenges in the system due
   to the physical phenomena used to obtain the entropy for the random
   number. On the other hand, the non-volatile memories request special
   technologies with additional processes and layers. This work presents an
   implementation of a One-Time Program (OTP) memory using an Anti-Fuse
   (AF) methodology in 180nm standard CMOS technology. In addition, a
   Physical Unclonable Function (PUF) is unified, exploiting the
   post-program current generated in the OTP bit cell. A 128-bit OTP array
   and high-voltage driver are implemented without any special process and
   layer, occupying 68000 mu m(2). On the other hand, the PUF
   implementation achieves 49.02\% of uniformity, 49.52\% of uniqueness,
   and 99.88\% of reliability in the worst case with Process, Voltage, and
   Temperature (PVT) variations. In addition, the PUF reports a
   2651F(2)/bit normalized area. The OTP memory application needs 6.7-V to
   program the AF bit cell.},
