Note = {5th Women in Technology Conference, INDIA, NOV 12-13, 2024},
Abstract = {Conventional testing methods struggle to ensure high quality in sub-10nm
   Integrated Circuits (ICs) for critical applications in automotive and
   healthcare. Shrinking geometries lead to two issues: (1) many defects
   within and between cells remain undetected by test patterns, and (2)
   fault grading becomes unreliable due to smaller defect signatures
   mimicking process variations. Cell-aware test (CAT) improves defect
   coverage, but some latent defects, especially in the lowest metal
   layers, remain hidden even under stress. These defects require elevated
   voltage stress for activation. Such latent defects require elevated
   voltage stress and the capability to generate and grade patterns based
   on fault activation for the defects to pop out and be detectable. In
   this paper, CAT pattern generation flow is oriented to target cell-aware
   test by generating and grading the right set of patterns based on fault
   activation under elevated voltage stress. This paper presents a novel
   and comprehensive approach for testing cell-aware defects under elevated
   voltage stress. It highlights the quality and reliability of the pattern
   grading under elevated Process, Voltage, and Temperature (PVT) corners
   in industrial designs to showcase increased performance, pattern count,
   and test coverage. The focus is on producing an optimal set of stress
   patterns that helps achieve maximum coverage within a practical
   timeframe. In summary, we observed an increase in defect coverage of
   similar to 24.02\% for 100 patterns across 9 designs under elevated
   voltage stress test compared to conventional IDDQ tests. The probability
   of defect occurrence would increase multi-fold in complex designs
   especially with multi-bit cells. This novel approach can be leveraged
   for such convoluted multi-bit cell designs.},
