   Exploiting Non-normalized Knuth-Yao Sampling and Inter-Core Register
   Sharing},
Booktitle = {2024 50TH IEEE EUROPEAN SOLID-STATE ELECTRONICS RESEARCH CONFERENCE,
   ESSERC 2024},
Series = {Proceedings of the European Solid-State Circuits Conference},
Year = {2024},
Pages = {29-32},
Note = {50th IEEE European Solid-State Electronics Research Conference (ESSERC),
   Bruges, BELGIUM, SEP 09-12, 2024},
Abstract = {Probabilistic graphical models (PMs) are popular to empower machine
   learning with the ability of reasoning and decision-making To perform
   approximate inference in PMs, sampling-based Markov Chain Monte Carlo
   (MCMC) algorithms are commonly employed. Unfortunately, MCMC is compute
   intensive and hard to run in parallel, resulting in inefficient
   execution on modern CPU/GPU platforms. This paper proposes AIA, an
   Approximate Inference Accelerator designed 1() empower decision-making
   and reasoning at the edge. ALA consists of a RISC-V host, and a 2D mesh
   of 16 customized RISC-V cores optimized 10 efficiently support PM
   inference, each featuring (i) a novel non-normalized Knuth-Yao sampler
   and interpolation unit; and (ii) core-to-core direct data access via the
   register file, which provides solutions for compute-intensive
   operations. To fully exploit the parallel potential of Markov Chain
   Monte Carlo (MCMC) algorithms, a customized compiler chain has been
   developed for effective spatial mapping and scheduling on the chip. ALA
   can generate 1277 MSample/s at 0.9V and 20 GSamples/s/W at 0.7V which is
   up to 2x faster and 1.45x more energy efficient compared 10 the previous
   slate-of-the-art Markov Random Field (MRF) accelerator. We further map
   Bayesian Networks benchinark onto AIA to show the flexibility of our
   design.},
